The fixed tool definition, as listed to the model:

{"name":"get_learner_enrollment","description":"Read the enrollment of the learner who opened the current ticket in one course (the learner comes from the ticket, never from you). Returns status, progress and purchase date, or found: false.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"course_slug":{"type":"string","description":"Course slug as in the course URL, e.g. react-hooks. Not an email, not a title: call search_courses first if you only have the title.","maxLength":80,"pattern":"^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$"}},"required":["course_slug"],"additionalProperties":false}}

Replay of ticket #9034 with a scripted model:

turn 1  model -> get_learner_enrollment {"course_slug":"minh.tran@example.com"}
        tool  -> error invalid_arguments: "course_slug" does not match pattern ^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$
        handler calls: 0
turn 2  model -> search_courses {"query":"React Hooks"}

Statement: "The handler never ran for the email, and the error the model received names the parameter and its expected format, which gives it what it needs to look up the slug instead of telling the learner he is not enrolled."

A first version of the validator stops at the first problem it finds. Trace for one ticket:

turn 4  model -> list_submissions {"challenge":"chal_ts_042","limit":"10","status":"passed"}
        tool  -> error: unexpected argument "challenge"
turn 5  model -> list_submissions {"challenge_id":"chal_ts_042","limit":"10","status":"passed"}
        tool  -> error: "limit" must be integer
turn 6  model -> list_submissions {"challenge_id":"chal_ts_042","limit":10,"status":"passed"}
        tool  -> error: "status" must be one of "accepted", "rejected", "pending"
turn 7  model -> list_submissions {"challenge_id":"chal_ts_042","limit":10,"status":"accepted"}
        tool  -> ok (3 rows)

input tokens per model call: turn 4: 6,900  turn 5: 7,300  turn 6: 7,650  turn 7: 8,020

Statement: "Stopping at the first violation is the cheaper design here: each error message is shorter, so these extra retries cost about the same as one retry that lists all three problems."
